{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothVariableSplit","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothVariableSplit","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothOperatorFamily","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothInductionHypothesis"],"declarations":42,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_three","kind":"theorem","line":988,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L988-L996","doc":"**An instance with nothing left open**: `t = 1/16 = (1/2)^{2²}`, so the ladder\n`1/16, 1/4, 1/2` lives in `ℚ` and §14 runs to `M = 3`.\n\n⚠ This is the arc's first statement of Roth's Lemma with **no hypothesis at all** beyond the\narity bound — no `Γ` to supply, no Lemma 4 to assume, no `s`-sequence to produce. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_of_seq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_of_seq","kind":"theorem","line":976,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L976-L986","doc":"**Roth's Lemma in `Θ` form, unconditional on Lemma 4.**\n\n⚠ The `s`-ladder here is correction 23's encoding restriction: `t` has to be a `2^{M−1}`-th\npower in `ℚ`.  **`QOU/Mathlib/RothRealLadder.lean` removes it** — `rothLemmaTheta_all` holds at\nevery arity for every `p, q, F`, by taking the roots in `ℝ` where they exist.  This form is kept\nbecause it is `ℝ`-free, and because `rothLemmaTheta_three` is a self-contained instance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemma4_of_one_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemma4_of_one_le","kind":"theorem","line":858,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L858-L969","doc":"**Lemma 4 (§13), proved.**\n\n`Γ_{n+1}` produces a descended system with `Γ_n` and `Θ_{n+1} ≤ t + 2·max(Ψ, √Ψ)` where\n`Ψ = Θ_n + t`.  No hypothesis beyond `0 < p ≤ q`, `1 ≤ n`, and `Γ_{n+1}` itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_w_of_rothGrowth_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_family_index_le_w_of_rothGrowth_split","kind":"theorem","line":769,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L769-L829","doc":"**§13 in Lemma 4's shape, transported** — the family exposed, the `w` disjunction, and\neverything stated in terms of `σ`, `o` and `d`.\n\nThis is the form `RothLemma4` consumes.  Compared with `index_le_of_rothGrowth_split`:\n\n* the realising family comes **back** rather than being quantified over, because the descended\n  system depends on the representation length `r` (see\n  `RothOperatorFamily.exists_family_index_le_of_rothGrowth`);\n* the conclusion is the `w` disjunction, with no `q` and no junction condition;\n* the degree bounds are stated against `splitLeft o d` / `splitRight o d`, which is what\n  `InRothPolySet` gives the caller, rather than against `P`'s own `degreeOf`.\n\n⚠ `hdegAll` replaces the single `hdeg` of the earlier form.  It is not a strengthening in\npractice — `A ∈ R(a; r)` bounds every degree — and the split-off variable's bound alone would\nnot give the `φ` and `ψ` degree clauses that the induction hypothesis needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rothGrowth_split","kind":"theorem","line":713,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L713-L767","doc":"**§13's conclusion, for a polynomial given as `o : σ ≃ Fin (n+1)` plus one degree vector.**\n\nThis is the shape `RothLemmaUniform` uses.  Everything about the `σ' ⊕ Unit` split is handled\ninternally; the caller states degrees, growth and the index bound entirely in terms of `σ`.\n\n⚠ `hres` is still phrased on the **renamed** polynomial, and deliberately: that is what the\ninduction actually produces, since the sub-problems live on the split.  Hiding the rename here\nwould only move the transport into the caller.\n\n⚠ It also carries the two non-vanishing hypotheses — see the tenth correction in\n`QOU/Mathlib/RothOperatorFamily.lean`.  Without them the hypothesis is unsatisfiable, because\nthe all-empty operator family is bounded-order, realises the same representation, and has a\nvanishing Wronskian for `r ≥ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder_inr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder_inr","kind":"theorem","line":703,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L703-L709","doc":"**`degreeOf`, transported, read at the right index.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder_inl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder_inl","kind":"theorem","line":695,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L695-L701","doc":"**`degreeOf`, transported, read at a left index.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_splitOrder","kind":"theorem","line":689,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L689-L693","doc":"**`degreeOf`, transported.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast_hlast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast_hlast","kind":"theorem","line":682,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L682-L687","doc":"**The split-off variable comes last** — the hypothesis `index_le_of_rothGrowth_*` needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothHeightFloor_two","kind":"theorem","line":678,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L678-L680","doc":"⚠ **`Γ_2` really does exclude `q = 2`** — the floor at arity 2 is `1024`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGamma_descend_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGamma_descend_witness","kind":"theorem","line":662,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L662-L676","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the descent**, and a demonstration that clause 3 bites.\n\n`n + 1 = 2`, `d ≡ 1`, `r = 1`, `ω = 1`.  ⚠ The heights cannot be `2`: at arity `2` the\nfloor is `2^⌈1·(1·2·5)⌉ = 2^10 = 1024`, so `Γ_2` demands `H₁ ≥ 1024`.  That is worth exhibiting\nrather than describing — every other witness in this arc got away with `q = 2`, and clause 3 is\nthe one hypothesis that makes that impossible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGammaFull_descend_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGammaFull_descend_witness","kind":"theorem","line":641,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L641-L660","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the full descent** — the largest hypothesis stack in this arc, satisfied.\n\nArity `2 → 1`, `t = 1` (`p = q = 1`), `F = 10`, `K = 1024 = 2^F`, `d ≡ 1`, `a = 1`, `r = 1`,\n`c = 5`, `b = 32`.  ⚠ As with `height_descend_pq_witness`, several hypotheses hold with\n**equality** — `2^F = K`, `q·(n(n+1)(2n+3)) = F·p`, `c = (2n+3)ρ₁`, `b = 2^c·a^r` — because\nclause 3's calibration leaves no room. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_succ_of_lemma4_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_succ_of_lemma4_witness","kind":"theorem","line":629,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L629-L639","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the induction step.**  `n = 1`, `s = 1/4`, so `t = 1/16`; sub-problem\nbound `Θ' = 1/16` (tight for `RothBound 1`), `Ψ = w = 1/8`, `Θ = 5/16`.\n\n⚠ Three hypotheses hold with **equality** — `Θ' = t`, `w = Ψ`, and `Θ = t + 2w` — so the\nwitness sits on the boundary of what the step allows, not comfortably inside it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_iterate_seq_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_iterate_seq_witness","kind":"theorem","line":608,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L608-L627","doc":"**Non-vacuity of the iteration's square-root sequence** — and the concrete shape of\ncorrection twenty-three's restriction.\n\nReaching arity `M` needs `M − 1` successive rational square roots, so `t` must be a\n`2^{M−1}`-th power.  At `M = 3` and `t = 1/16 = (1/2)^{2²}` the sequence is\n`1/16, 1/4, 1/2` and stops exactly where it is needed.\n\n⚠ One more root — `M = 4` at the same `t` — would need `√(1/2)`, which is irrational.  So the\nwitness is at the boundary: it exists **because** the range is bounded, and the unbounded form\ncorrection twenty-three removed had no non-constant instance at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_iterate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_iterate","kind":"theorem","line":570,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L570-L606","doc":"**§14's recursion, iterated** — `RothLemma4` at every arity from `1` to `M−1`, plus the base\ncase, gives `RothLemmaTheta` at every `m ≤ M`.\n\nThe square-root sequence is `s` with `s 0 = t` and `s (n+1)² = s n`, so `s n ^ {2^n} = t` at\nevery level — the `s` each application of `rothLemmaTheta_succ` needs.  Correction twenty-three\nis exactly the observation that such a rational sequence exists only for `t` a `2^{M−1}`-th\npower; bounding the range to `n < M` is what makes it exist at all.\n\n⚠ A first draft of *this* theorem left `hsq` unbounded — reproducing correction twenty-three's\nown defect one commit after fixing it, which is worth recording because it says something about\nthe failure mode: the unbounded form is what one writes by default, and nothing objects until\nsomeone asks whether the hypothesis is satisfiable.\n\n⚠ The base case is `rothLemmaTheta_one_fin` — `rothLemmaTheta_one` (proved at `Unit`, because\nthe `m = 1` chain runs through `pUnitAlgEquiv`) carried to `Fin 1` by `RothLemmaTheta.rename_equiv`.\nAn earlier version took it as a hypothesis, because the transport did not exist; it does now. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_succ","kind":"theorem","line":545,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L545-L568","doc":"**§14's recursion, one step** — `RothLemma4` at arity `n+1` plus `RothLemmaTheta` at `n`\ngives `RothLemmaTheta` at `n+1`.\n\nThis is the induction the whole arc has been building toward, and with the pieces in place it is\nfour lines: Lemma 4 hands over a sub-problem with `Γ_{n}` and its own `Θ'`, the induction\nhypothesis turns that `Θ'` into a `RothBound`, and `rothBound_succ_of_lemma4` does §14's\narithmetic.\n\n⚠ **`s` is a hypothesis, and that is correction twenty-three showing through.**\n`rothBound_succ_of_lemma4` needs a *rational* `s` with `t = s^{2^n}`, because the root-free\nencoding cannot take square roots.  For the recursion to run from `1` to `m` one needs such an\n`s` at every level, i.e. `t` a `2^{m−1}`-th power in `ℚ`.  §14 has no such restriction — over `ℝ`\nevery `t ≥ 0` has the roots — so this is an artifact of the encoding, and removing it means\nproving the step in `ℝ` and transferring back.  Not done; the hypothesis is explicit so that the\nrestriction is visible at every use rather than buried. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothBound_succ_of_lemma4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothBound_succ_of_lemma4","kind":"theorem","line":508,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L508-L543","doc":"**§14's induction step in `Θ` language** — Lemma 4's conclusion plus the sub-problem's\n`RothBound` gives the next one.\n\nBoth levels sit at the *same* `t`: level `n` uses the variable `s²` and level `n+1` uses `s`,\nand `(s²)^{2^{n−1}} = s^{2^n}`.  That is what makes this a single-parameter induction.\n\nThe three inputs are exactly Lemma 4's: the sub-problem's bound `RothBound n t Θ'`, the\n`max(Ψ, √Ψ)` disjunct, and `Θ ≤ t + 2w`.  ⚠ The leading term is `t`, not `s` — Lemma 4 leads\nwith the row cost — and `t ≤ s` is what lets `rothConstant_step_lemma4` accept it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_one_fin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_one_fin","kind":"theorem","line":498,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L498-L506","doc":"**The base case at `Fin 1`.**  `rothLemmaTheta_one` proves it at `Unit`; `finOneEquiv`\ncarries it across.\n\n⚠ This closes a gap `rothLemmaTheta_iterate` used to carry as a hypothesis.  It was worth\ncarrying rather than hiding: the two propositions really are different, and the transport needed\n`index_rename_equiv` plus new transports for `InRothPolySet`, `InRothFracSet` and\n`RothGammaFull`.  \"The variable type is incidental\" is true and is still four lemmas. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothLemmaTheta.rename_equiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothLemmaTheta.rename_equiv","kind":"theorem","line":488,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L488-L496","doc":"**`RothLemmaTheta` transports along a type equivalence.**\n\nComposing the equivalence into the ordering (`e.symm.trans o`) and into the degree, height and\npoint data is all it takes, once `RothGammaFull` and `IsRothTheta` transport. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothLemmaTheta_one","kind":"theorem","line":468,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L468-L486","doc":"**Roth's lemma at `m = 1`, in `Θ` form** — `RothLemmaTheta` discharged at the base.\n\nStraight from `isRothTheta_le_of_gamma_one`: `RothGammaFull`'s `heightBound` at `m = 1` is\n`a^{1·q} ≤ K₁^{r₁p}`, which is §11's case (i) cleared of its exponent, and `two_le` supplies\ncase (i)'s `K₁ ≥ 2`.  `rothBound_one` turns `Θ ≤ t` into `RothBound 1 t Θ`.\n\nThis is the first instance of `RothLemmaTheta` proved, and it is the value `rothConstant_chain`\nneeds to start from: correction eleven traced `c_m = 2^{m+1} − 3` to the base case rather than\nthe recurrence, and `c₁ = 1` is exactly `Θ₁ ≤ t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothLemmaTheta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothLemmaTheta","kind":"def","line":422,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L422-L466","doc":"**Roth's Lemma**, stated in `Θ_m` form: `Γ_m` forces `Θ_m ≤ c_m·t^{2^{−(m−1)}}`,\nwith the conclusion in `RothBound`'s root-free shape.\n\n**This is the shape the induction actually uses**, and §12 settles that it is: at the point\nwhere the induction hypothesis is invoked, Mahler writes\n\n> `J(U; ρ₁,…,ρ_{m−1}; κ₁,…,κ_{m−1}) ≤ Θ_{m−1}(b; ρ₁,…,ρ_{m−1}; H₁,…,H_{m−1})`,\n> `J(V; ρ_m; κ_m) ≤ Θ₁(b; ρ_m; H_m)`\n\n— both by the **definition** of `Θ` (`U ∈ R(b;ρ)`, `κ ∈ Q(H)`), with no exponent condition\nanywhere.  The `Γ` clauses enter separately: §11's descent proves `Γ_{m−1}` for the descended\nsystem, and *that* is what lets the induction bound `Θ_{m−1}`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction nineteen: `RothLemmaUniform` cannot serve as the induction hypothesis.**  It\nfuses the two — membership in `R(b;ρ)×Q(H)` and `Γ` — into one `∀`-statement sharing a single\n`E` with `∀ j, B ≤ Kh j ^ E` and `∀ j, E ≤ ε·d j`.  §11 states clause 4 only at `h = 1`\n(`b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}`); clause 2 (`K_h^{s_h} ≥ K₁^{s₁}`) propagates it as `b ≤ K_h^{ε s_h}`, an\nexponent that **varies with `h`**.  A single `E ≤ ε·min_h d_h` does not follow — `E ≤ ε s_h`\nmakes `K_h^E ≤ K_h^{ε s_h}`, the wrong direction.  So `RothLemmaUniform` is true but unusable\nat its point of use, exactly like the pre-correction-18b form was.  `RothLemmaTheta` has no `E`\nand is what §12 invokes.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction twenty: this was false at `m = 1`, and the counterexample is small.**\n`RothGammaFull` encodes `Γ_M`'s case (ii), which requires `M ≥ 2`; at `M = 1` the source uses\ncase (i), `M = 1, K₁ ≥ 2, b ≤ K₁^{s₁t}`.  Nothing in case (ii) forces `K₁ ≥ 2` when `m = 1`,\nbecause clause 3a reads `q·((1−1)·1·3) ≤ F·p`, i.e. `0 ≤ 0`, leaving `F = 0` admissible and\nclause 3b saying only `1 ≤ K₁`.  Take\n\n    σ = Unit,  m = 1,  p = 1,  q = 2  (t = 1/2),  F = 0,  a = 1,  r ≡ 1,  H ≡ 1.\n\nEvery clause holds: growth is vacuous at one variable, `height` is `1 ≤ 1`, `floorExp` is\n`0 ≤ 0`, `floor` is `2^0 = 1 ≤ 1`, `heightBound` is `1^2 = 1 ≤ 1^1 = 1`.  And `Θ = 1`, attained\nat `A = x − 1`, `κ = 1` (height `max(1,1) = 1`, so `κ ∈ Q(1)`), whose translate is `x`.  The\nconclusion `RothBound 1 (1/2) 1` asks `1 ≤ 1/2`.\n\nFixed by the `two_le` clause on `RothGammaFull` and by carrying §14's opening `0 < t ≤ 1` —\nwhich `RothLemmaMahler` already carried and this did not.  `q = 0` is the second half of that:\nit makes `t = 0` in `ℚ` while `heightBound` degenerates to `1 ≤ K₁^{r₁p}`, so `Θ ≤ 0` is\ndemanded and false (take `a = 100`, `H = 2`, `A = 2x − 1`, `κ = 1/2`, giving `Θ ≥ 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.lemma4_le_encoding_needed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.lemma4_le_encoding_needed","kind":"theorem","line":400,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L400-L420","doc":"**Correction 25, made concrete** — §13's conclusion holds at numbers the `=` encoding of\n`max(Ψ, √Ψ)` cannot express, and the `≤` encoding expresses exactly.\n\nTake `Ψ = 1/2`, `t = 0`, `Θ = 7/5`.  §13's second branch is `(Θ − t)² ≤ 4Ψ`, and here that is\n`49/25 ≤ 2` — true, so this is a situation §13 genuinely produces (it is the literal shape\n`RothInductionStep.index_le_or_sq_of_det_bound` hands back).  Then:\n\n* **no** rational `w` has `w = Ψ ∨ w² = Ψ` together with `Θ ≤ t + 2w` — the first disjunct\n  gives `t + 2w = 1 < 7/5`, and the second asks for a rational square root of `1/2`;\n* `w = 7/10` has `w² = 49/100 ≤ 1/2` and `Θ ≤ t + 2w` with equality.\n\nSo the change from `=` to `≤` is not a loosening of Lemma 4 — it is the difference between a\nstatement Lemma 4 can prove and one it cannot. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.not_isSquare_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.not_isSquare_half","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L386-L398","doc":"**`1/2` is not a square in `ℚ`** — elementary, via denominators.\n\n`(w²).den = w.den²` and `(1/2).den = 2`, so a solution would need a natural number whose\nsquare is `2`.  No analysis, no irrationality file: this is the whole obstruction correction 25\nis about, and it is visible entirely inside `ℚ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.RothLemma4","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.RothLemma4","kind":"def","line":342,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L342-L384","doc":"**Lemma 4**, stated.  `w` encodes `max(Ψ, √Ψ)` root-free as `w ≤ Ψ ∨ w² ≤ Ψ`.\n\n⚠⚠ **Correction twenty-five: the disjuncts were `=`, and that made this `def` unsatisfiable.**\nThe admissible `w` under `=` are `Ψ` and — only when `Ψ` is a square in `ℚ` — `±√Ψ`, so in the\nregime where the `√` branch is the operative one (`Ψ < 1`, which is the regime §14 runs in) the\nstatement demands a rational square root of `Θ' + t` that nothing produces.  Under `≤` the\nadmissible `w` are exactly `w ≤ max(Ψ, √Ψ)`, so this existential says precisely what §13 says,\nand `w := max(0, (Θ − t)/2)` is the witness §13's second branch hands over.\n\n⚠ The consumers never needed the equality: `rothConstant_step_lemma4` uses `w ≤ Ψ` and\n`w² ≤ Ψ`, and `radicand_weaken` uses `S/n ≤ w²`.  So the `=` was strictly a tax on the\nproducer, and it went unpaid for as long as nothing tried to produce one — correction 9's\nlesson again.\n⚠⚠ **Correction twenty-two: checked against correction twenty, and it was defective.**\n\nCorrection twenty's *first* half — `K₁ ≥ 2` — is fixed for free by `RothGammaFull`'s new `two_le`\nclause.  The *second* half is not, and it bites here at `n = 0` (arity `1`, where the source's\nLemma 4 does not apply at all — §13 assumes `m ≥ 2`).\n\nAt `n = 0` the sub-problem lives on `Fin 0`, where every index is `0` — `idxWeight` sums over an\nempty support — so `IsRothTheta b ρ H' Θ'` forces `Θ' = 0` (and `b ≥ 1`, else `R(b;ρ)` is empty\nand the attainment clause fails).  Then `w = Θ' + t = t` or `w² = t`.  With `q = 0` Lean's `ℚ`\nmakes `t = p/0 = 0`, so `w = 0` and the conclusion demands `Θ ≤ 0`.  But:\n\n    σ = Unit, n = 0, p = 1, q = 0, F = 0, a = 100, r ≡ 1, H ≡ 2\n\nsatisfies every `RothGammaFull` clause — `growth` vacuous at one variable, `height` `2 ≤ 2`,\n`floorExp` `0 ≤ 0`, `floor` `2⁰ = 1 ≤ 2`, `heightBound` `100^0 = 1 ≤ 2¹`, `two_le` `2 ≤ 2`,\n`degPos` `1 ≠ 0` — while `Θ = 1`, attained at `A = 2x − 1`, `κ = 1/2` (height `max(1,2) = 2`),\nwhose translate is `2x`.  So `1 ≤ 0` is demanded.\n\nFixed the same way as `RothLemmaTheta`: carry §14's opening `0 < t ≤ 1`.  With `q ≥ p ≥ 1` the\n`n = 0` case then asks only `Θ ≤ 3t`, which `rothLemmaTheta_one`'s `Θ ≤ t` supplies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_right_le_t","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.isRothTheta_right_le_t","kind":"theorem","line":257,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L257-L310","doc":"**§13's `Θ₁ ≤ t` for the split-off variable** — the `y` side of `Φ_m ≤ Ψ_m`.\n\n§13 needs `Φ_m = Θ_{m−1} + Θ₁ ≤ Θ_{m−1} + t = Ψ_m`, i.e. `Θ₁ ≤ t` for the *one-variable*\nsystem carrying the split-off degree.  `isRothTheta_le_of_gamma_one` turns §11's case (i) into\nexactly that, so all this has to do is produce case (i)'s clause `b^q ≤ K_M^{(r₀·d_M)·p}` for\nthe **last** variable, where `rothGammaFull_descend` produced it for the first.\n\nThat direction is the easy one, and for a reason worth naming: clause 2 says the *first*\nvariable's `K^d` is the **smallest**, so the last variable's clause is implied by the first's.\nRaising `K_1^{d_1} ≤ K_M^{d_M}` to `r₀·p` and prefixing `b^q ≤ b^{n·q}` closes it.\n\n⚠ `1 ≤ n` is used twice and both times essentially: for `q ≤ n·q`, and because at `n = 0` the\ndescended system has no first variable to read clause 4 off.  §13 assumes `m ≥ 2`, which is\n`n ≥ 1` here — the same hypothesis correction 22 had to add to `RothLemma4` for a different\nreason. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGammaFull_descend","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGammaFull_descend","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L187-L255","doc":"**§11's descent lemma, in full** — all four clauses of `Γ_{m}` from `Γ_{m+1}`.\n\nThis is what Lemma 4 asserts exists and what `rothGamma_descend` supplies only three quarters\nof.  The sub-system is §11's: first `n` variables, degrees rescaled by `r` (`ρ_h = r·s_h`),\nheights unchanged, and the new coefficient bound `b` bounded by `2^c·a^r` — which is\n`b = 2^{2r∑s}·r!·a^r` with `c` absorbing the first two factors.\n\nWhere each clause comes from:\n\n* **1–3** as in `rothGamma_descend`; clause 3a is now a `ℕ` inequality and descends by\n  monotonicity of `q·(M−1)M(2M+1)` in `M`, with no ceiling in the way.\n* **4** is `height_descend_pq`, and its hypothesis `hF` is *literally* `Γ_{n+1}`'s `floorExp`:\n  `q·((n+1−1)(n+1)(2(n+1)+1)) = q·(n(n+1)(2n+3))`.  The two were derived independently — one\n  from §11's height chain, one from §11's floor clause — and they are the same inequality.\n\n⚠ `hc : c ≤ (2n+3)·(r·s₁)` is the one hypothesis about the *construction* rather than about\n`Γ`: it says the accumulated `2`-power in `b` is what §11 says it is.  Discharging it is\n`WronskianHeight`'s business. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGamma_descend","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGamma_descend","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L127-L185","doc":"**`Γ_m` descends to `Γ_{m−1}` on the sub-system** — the piece Lemma 4 asserts exists.\n\nLemma 4 does not merely conclude an inequality: it produces an ordered system\n`b; ρ₁,…,ρ_{m−1}; H₁,…,H_{m−1}` **with property `Γ_{m−1}`**, and that is what lets the\ninduction apply its hypothesis at all.  Nothing in this arc supplied it until now — the gap\nwent unnoticed because every earlier step consumed `Γ_m` and none had to *produce* one.\n\nThe sub-system is §13's: the first `m−1` variables, degrees rescaled by `n` (`ρ_h = n·r_h`,\nread off §13's body), **heights unchanged** — §13 descends to `b, ρ₁,…,ρ_{m−1}, H₁,…,H_{m−1}`,\nthe same `H` truncated by one.  All three clauses come across:\n\n* **growth** — restricting the consecutive-pair condition to a prefix loses nothing, and the\n  rescaling is `rothGrowth_scale`'s;\n* **height** — clause 2 compares every `h` against `h = 1`, so a prefix keeps its own first\n  element; raising to the `r`-th power is monotone once `2 ≤ qⱼ`;\n* **floor** — `rothHeightFloor` is monotone in the arity, so the *larger* floor `Γ_m` supplies\n  implies the smaller one `Γ_{m−1}` asks for.\n\n⚠ Only the arity moves.  `ω` is the same `t⁻¹` throughout, which is what makes this a\nsingle-parameter induction — and is why `rothHeightFloor_mono` needs no hypothesis relating two\ndifferent `ω`s.\n\n⚠⚠ **This produces clauses 1–3 and *not* clause 4**, and that is where the remaining work is.\n`Γ_M`'s fourth clause is `b ≤ K₁^{(1/M)s₁t}` — a bound on the sub-system's **new height** `b`,\nwhich is the Wronskian's.  Nothing about `Γ_m` can supply it: `Γ_m` constrains `a`, the\n*original* height, and `b` is a different number that §13 has to estimate.\n\nThat relocates what this arc had been calling \"the height chain, which\n`theta_one_le_of_gamma` consumes\".  It is not a lemma feeding that theorem — it is\n`Γ_{m−1}`'s own clause 4, and `theta_one_le_of_gamma` consumes it *because* the sub-system\nhas `Γ_{m−1}`.  So the open item is: **produce clause 4 for the Wronskian**, which is\n`WronskianHeight`'s business and the one genuinely unbuilt piece left. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLeft_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLeft_apply","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L121-L125","doc":"`splitLeft` in terms of the original ordering: position `j` of `Fin n` is position `j` of\n`Fin (n+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_splitOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.rothGrowth_splitOrder","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L108-L119","doc":"**The growth condition, transported.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_splitOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_splitOrder","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L100-L106","doc":"**The index, transported.**  One rewriting of `index_rename_equiv`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitRight_unit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitRight_unit","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L96-L98","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.split_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.split_eq","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L91-L94","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitRight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitRight","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L87-L89","doc":"The right half — a one-element family, the split-off variable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLeft","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLeft","kind":"def","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L83-L85","doc":"The left half of a `σ`-indexed function, read through the split. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitOrder_symm_inr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitOrder_symm_inr","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L73-L76","doc":"The split-off variable: the last one in the ordering. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitOrder_symm_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitOrder_symm_apply","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L67-L71","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitOrder","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L62-L65","doc":"The variable equivalence an ordering induces: the last variable becomes the `Unit`\ncomponent, and every other variable keeps its position. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast_symm_inr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast_symm_inr","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L58-L60","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast_symm_inl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast_symm_inl","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L54-L56","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast_last","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast_last","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L51-L52","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast_castSucc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast_castSucc","kind":"theorem","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L47-L49","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.splitLast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.splitLast","kind":"def","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothVariableSplit.lean#L43-L45","doc":"`Fin (n+1) ≃ Fin n ⊕ Unit`, sending `Fin.last` to the `Unit` component. ","signatureChunk":0}]}